
What problem does this solve? attacker flood the switch with random MACs to force the switch to broadcast trafic to all devices on the network, the switch is turned into a hub

Key terms & 1-liners:-
- CAM table: switch memory of MACs
- each device have a unique MAC

How its done:
an attacker sends a huge number of frames with fake/unique source MAC addresses to fill the CAM table. When the CAM table is full or overwhelmed, the switch can no longer learn new MAC→port mappings reliably and may fail open — i.e., it starts flooding frames out all ports (behaving like a hub). That lets the attacker sniff traffic for other hosts on the same VLAN.

Detection signs:
High rate of MAC table churn — many MACs learned then aged out quickly.

MAC flapping warnings/logs (same MAC appearing on different ports repeatedly).

Unexplained traffic sniffing alerts or many hosts suddenly receiving broadcasts they normally don’t.

CPU or forwarding performance drops on the switch.

Unusual syslog/SNMP alerts (if you monitor MAC-table size changes).




Practical mitigations (what to configure)

These are defensive configs you should learn and apply:

Port Security

Limit the number of MACs allowed per access port (e.g., maximum 1–2).

Use sticky MAC where possible so the switch learns and sticks the legitimate MAC.

Configure the port action on violation (shutdown or restrict) rather than simply logging.

Disable unused ports and place them in an isolated VLAN before enabling them if needed.

Storm control / broadcast suppression

Throttle excessive broadcast/multicast floods to protect CPU/forwarding.

802.1X (port-based access control)

Require authentication for devices before normal Layer-2 access.

VLAN segmentation / Private VLANs

Keep untrusted devices in separate restricted VLANs.

DHCP snooping + Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI)

These limit ARP/DHCP-based malicious activity which often pairs with layer-2 attacks.

Logging & monitoring

Monitor MAC learning rates, MAC table usage, and set alerts via syslog/SNMP/NetFlow.

Track MAC flapping and unusual port activity.

Switch firmware & features

Use hardware that supports CAM limits, secure defaults, and port-security features.




How to practice safely in a lab (legal & educational)

Do this only in a controlled lab you own (Packet Tracer, GNS3, EVE-NG, or real lab switches you control).

Set up topology: a switch, an attacker host (VM), a victim host (VM), a management host. Put them in the same VLAN.

Baseline: observe the CAM table, normal traffic, and verify reachability between hosts.

Enable defensive settings (port security, storm control, 802.1X if supported) on the access ports. Confirm expected behavior for normal multi-host scenarios.

Simulate load safely: use traffic-generator functionality inside the lab to create many source MACs only as a lab exercise (or simply connect many VMs). The objective is to observe how the switch reacts and how your port security and monitoring catch it — do not attempt this on any production network.

Record logs & alerts: check syslog, SNMP traps, and MAC-table stats to learn detection signals.
